# Pre-audit deep gate — Remit v2, forge-std 1.16.2
#
# Command:  FOUNDRY_PROFILE=deep forge test
#           (env-var form, not --profile. This Foundry rejects that flag outright —
#            "error: unexpected argument '--profile' found" — and on a build that
#            accepted and ignored it the gate would run the default profile without
#            saying so, which is the failure this form exists to prevent)
# Date:     2026-09-05, started 10:12:38Z, finished 10:21:59Z
#           (clock times read from this log's own mtime and from coverage.log's, which the
#            same paste wrote immediately before this run started. They agree with `time`'s
#            wall figure to the second, so they are derived rather than guessed)
# Result:   320 passed, 0 failed, 0 skipped, exit 0
# Wall:     real 9m21.166s, user 51m27.362s, sys 0m1.530s (15 suites, ~5.5x parallel)
#           Foundry self-reports 560.24s wall, 1799.40s CPU
#
# This supersedes deep-v3.log as the current gate. The three earlier logs stay where they
# are: deep-v3.log is the 278-test gate, deep-v2.log the 276-test gate, and deep.log is
# v1's gate at 140 tests against forge-std 1.9.6, the campaign the deployed bytecode at
# 0x3744E93B9e796E05CB66311d897559B6F3860196 was cleared by.
#
# What changed, and what did not. F51 added a payer-nominated revoker, a 42-test suite in
# test/Revoker.t.sol, and a fifteenth suite to the run. It added no handler move, so the
# invariant campaign is the same one deep-v3.log ran: three invariants, four moves, 2000
# runs at depth 256 for 512,000 calls each, and 12 rows across the per-selector tables.
#
# The tables below are the proof of that. All three carry one distribution — donate
# 128,136, idle 128,225, spendAll 128,007, spendSome 127,632, summing to exactly 512,000 —
# because a single generated corpus is replayed against every invariant. deep-v3.log's
# header quotes one donate figure without saying that; its three tables are identical too,
# at 128,102 / 127,425 / 127,872 / 128,601. Spend attempts here are 255,639 against
# deep-v3.log's 256,473, a fall of 834 calls or 0.33%, which is the random selector draw
# moving between runs rather than a further share going elsewhere. The reverts column
# stays the one to read, because `fail_on_revert = false` would have discarded a call from
# a drained purse and printed nothing at all about it.
#
# This run beats deep-v3.log by 87.70s, 560.24s against 647.94s, while carrying 42 more
# tests. `time`'s real figure agrees, 9m21.166s against 10m50.560s. The obvious
# explanation is ruled out: both runs open with "No files changed, compilation skipped",
# so neither paid for a compile, and nothing in the campaign changed either. That leaves
# the machine as the only candidate, with no measurement here able to name it. Recorded
# rather than explained, on the same footing as the v1 to v2 speedup that deep-v2.log left
# open.
#
# The pair of numbers deep-v3.log could not reconcile has narrowed and survived. Measured
# user time is 3087.362s against Foundry's own CPU figure of 1799.40s, a ratio of 1.72,
# where deep-v3.log had 3331.435s against 1382.23s for a ratio of 2.41. Both runs' wall
# figures agree with `time` to within a second, so the disagreement sits in the CPU
# accounting alone. Recorded, not explained.
#
# The forge config dump below is the CONTROL: it is what proves the deep profile was
# actually resolved. Look for fuzz runs = 20000, invariant runs = 2000, depth = 256,
# shrink_run_limit = 20000, and optimizer_runs = 200 matching the default profile. A gate
# that compiles different bytecode is not a gate. The dump is byte-identical to
# deep-v3.log's, which `diff` across the whole section reports without a line: 206 lines and
# 160 keys by `grep -c ' = '`, the same keys carrying the same values. deep-v3.log could
# claim only the weaker half of that, an unchanged key set with no value compared against
# deep-v2.log, so the resolved configuration is ruled out as a variable in the 87.70s
# difference recorded above, and so is the Foundry build behind it.
#
